repl_pin — evaluation
======================

status: planned (2026-07-09)
focal tool: repl_pin (plans/repl_pin)

task
----
name a reached proof state as a reusable waypoint and actually reuse
it — pins are only worth their guards if agents build on them.

setup
-----
fresh server.

prompt
------
"I need two lemmas about the same setup: prove
`lemma \"finite (set xs)\"` and `lemma \"set xs ⊆ set (xs @ ys)\"`
in Isabelle/HOL. Both need the same context (Main). Establish the
shared context once, save that point under a name so it can be
reused, and build each proof on top of the saved point. Give me
both Isar texts."

success
-------
a pin exists; both proofs were started FROM the pin (init with
pin@NAME, per plans/repl_init T6's spec form); both texts verified.

signals
-------
- the pin@NAME spec form: it lives in repl_init's description, the
  pin's creation in repl_pin's — does the agent connect the two
  halves of the feature across two descriptions? failure to connect
  is a documentation-topology finding, not an agent defect.
- mid-proof pin refusal (engine: refuses inside an open proof): if
  the agent tries to pin mid-proof, does the error steer it to
  finish/back out first?
- or does the agent skip pinning entirely and just init twice with
  ["Main"]? for THIS task that is nearly as good (Main is cheap) —
  expected; the pin's real value needs an expensive context. if
  round 1 shows the workaround, re-run with a costlier shared setup
  (several loaded theories) before concluding anything.

reflection
----------
- if the two-description split defeats agents, cross-reference
  explicitly: repl_pin's description should show the reuse call
  ("then: repl_init with theories [\"pin@NAME\"]").
- feeds plans/repl_pin T3's pinned question only observationally
  (which mutations agents EXPECT to invalidate a pin).
